23. Vicious Circle of Poverty and Structural View of Underdevelopment
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: I | Topic: Perpetuation of underdevelopment – vicious circle of poverty, structural view of underdevelopment
Introduction
Why does underdevelopment persist? Two classic explanations are Nurkse's vicious circle of poverty and the structuralist view of institutional and structural rigidities.
Vicious circle of poverty
Ragnar Nurkse (1953): "a country is poor because it is poor." Circular constellations of forces tend to perpetuate low living standards.
• Supply side: low income leads to low savings, then low investment, low capital stock, low productivity and back to low income.
• Demand side: low income leads to small market, low inducement to invest, low capital formation, low productivity and low income.
• Other circles: poverty leads to poor health and education, low human capital and low productivity; underdeveloped markets for capital; population pressure.
Breaking the circle requires a big push: a large-scale, simultaneous investment (Rosenstein-Rodan), balanced growth across sectors (Nurkse), or foreign aid and forced savings.
Critique: Poor countries have grown (Japan, Korea, China). Circles ignore institutions and policy and exaggerate savings constraints. But they explain poverty traps and multiple equilibria, now formalised in modern models (Azariadis, Sachs).
Structural view of underdevelopment
Structuralists (Prebisch, Furtado, Myrdal, Lewis) see underdevelopment not as an initial stage but as a distinct structure:
1. Dualism: a small modern sector coexists with a large traditional one with surplus labour.
2. Sectoral imbalance: reliance on primary exports, a weak industrial base and low intersectoral linkages.
3. Inelastic supply in agriculture and infrastructure bottlenecks, causing inflation.
4. External dependence: deteriorating terms of trade, import needs and foreign exchange constraints.
5. Unequal income distribution limits the domestic market.
6. Institutional rigidities: land tenure, weak states, social hierarchies.
The policy implication is state-led structural transformation: industrialisation, import substitution, land reform and planning.
Comparison
The vicious circle is primarily an economic, savings-and-demand explanation of a trap; the structural view broadens it to institutions, sectoral composition and international relations.
Conclusion
Both views highlight why underdevelopment is self-reinforcing and why deliberate policy is needed to move an economy to a higher equilibrium.